  "query_preview": "..(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 342 AM EDT SAT SEP 19 2026\n\nA rather chaotic upper level setup is anticipated across the CONUS \nto start the long term period. The main belt of prevailing \nwesterlies aloft will be well to our north, largely in Canada. South \nof that, a number of features semi-disconnected from the main flow \nwill be over the CONUS, but with largely zonal flow over the Ohio \nValley and little flow to the south. A surface frontal boundary \nshould be stalled over or near KY along the southern edge of the \nflow aloft. This boundary could serve as a broad focus for \nconvective development Sunday night into Monday. \n\nThe most significant feature aloft for our local area at the start \nof the period will be a trough (and possibly weak low) over the \nnorthern plains. This feature traces back to a current position over \nID. As it drifts east, models are in relatively good agreement \nthrough Wednesday for it to form a larger closed low near the Great \nLakes. The resulting trough over the eastern CONUS will support a \ntransition to cooler weather, but the details of potential fropa for \nthe local area are a little messy, as the cooler air is expected to \nmake better progress down the eastern side of the Appalachians. This \npotentially pinches the warmer air mass along the western side here. \nWe should see an increased POP with the transition in air masses \naround mid week, but the details are murky as to how/when a full \ntransition eventually takes place. Model solutions also begin to \nshow more variation with the evolution of the upper low at the end \nof the week, particularly in the way it shifts east and with ridging \ntrying to build east toward the Great Lakes. This lowers confidence \nin the forecast at the end of the period.",
